# database-migrations (Cursor)

## Core principles

Every change is a migration — never alter production databases manually.
Migrations are forward-only in production (rollback = a new forward
migration). Schema and data migrations stay separate (never mix DDL and DML
in one migration). Test against production-sized data before applying —
what works on 100 rows can lock on 10M. Never edit a migration that has
already run in production.

## Safety checklist before applying

Both UP and DOWN present (or explicitly irreversible); no full table locks
on large tables (use concurrent index creation); new columns nullable or
have a default (never `NOT NULL` without a default on an existing table —
it forces a full rewrite); backfill is a separate migration from the schema
change; tested against production-sized data; rollback plan documented.

## Safe patterns

- Adding a column: nullable, or `NOT NULL DEFAULT` on Postgres 11+ (instant,
  no rewrite). Never bare `NOT NULL` on an existing table.
- Adding an index on a large table: `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` (can't run
  inside a transaction block — most ORMs need special handling).
- Renaming or removing a column: never directly. Use expand-contract — add
  new column, backfill, deploy app reading/writing both, then drop old
  column in a later migration once app code no longer references it.
- Large data migrations: batch updates (e.g. `LIMIT`+`FOR UPDATE SKIP
  LOCKED` loop), never a single unbounded `UPDATE` that locks the whole
  table.

## Zero-downtime (expand-contract)

Phase 1 EXPAND: add new column/table, deploy app writing to both old and
new, backfill. Phase 2 MIGRATE: deploy app reading from new, writing both,
verify consistency. Phase 3 CONTRACT: deploy app using new only, drop old
in a separate migration.

## Per-tool workflow

Prisma: `migrate dev` (create), `migrate deploy` (apply), custom SQL via
`migrate dev --create-only` for anything Prisma can't express (concurrent
indexes). Drizzle: `drizzle-kit generate`/`migrate`. Kysely: `kysely
migrate make/latest/down`, always type migrations as `Kysely<any>` since
they're frozen in time. Django: `makemigrations`/`migrate`,
`SeparateDatabaseAndState` to drop a model field without touching the DB
yet. golang-migrate: paired `.up.sql`/`.down.sql` files.

## Anti-patterns

Manual SQL in production, editing deployed migrations, `NOT NULL` without
default, inline index on a large table, schema+data in one migration,
dropping a column before removing the code that references it.
